WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.933
Object-level authorization defects live in
forbidden paths,

2
00:00:04.013 --> 00:00:07.057
so happy-path tests cannot demonstrate
their absence.

3
00:00:07.137 --> 00:00:11.865
Build reusable fixtures with two users and
objects in distinct authorization scopes.

4
00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:15.054
Exercise reads, updates, deletes,
downloads, exports,

5
00:00:15.134 --> 00:00:17.919
and actions while authenticated as the
wrong peer.

6
00:00:17.999 --> 00:00:20.525
Repeat for the same role in different
tenants,

7
00:00:20.605 --> 00:00:25.334
privileged and non-privileged subjects,
nested resources, alternate identifiers,

8
00:00:25.414 --> 00:00:26.126
and batches.

9
00:00:26.206 --> 00:00:30.805
Test UUIDs with known valid values because
the control must hold after an identifier

10
00:00:30.885 --> 00:00:31.273
leaks.

11
00:00:31.353 --> 00:00:35.822
Represent expected decisions as an
authorization matrix when policy has several

12
00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:38.364
roles, relationships, actions, and states.

13
00:00:38.444 --> 00:00:40.840
Unit tests can cover pure policy
functions.

14
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.065
Repository tests can verify scoped
predicates and affected-row behavior.

15
00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:49.744
Integration tests should make real
requests across identities and confirm both the

16
00:00:49.824 --> 00:00:51.897
response and absence of side effects.

17
00:00:51.977 --> 00:00:55.086
For a denied update, verify that the
record is unchanged.

18
00:00:55.166 --> 00:00:56.720
For a denied export or batch,

19
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.464
verify that no unauthorized item appears
and no unintended partial mutation occurs.

20
00:01:01.544 --> 00:01:04.912
Code review should trace each reference
from input to effect.

21
00:01:04.992 --> 00:01:07.259
Identify where the object ID originated,

22
00:01:07.339 --> 00:01:10.188
what binds the object to the authenticated
subject,

23
00:01:10.268 --> 00:01:14.673
where tenant context came from, and
whether the same scope reaches the mutation.

24
00:01:14.753 --> 00:01:18.833
Check routes that address the resource by
slug, filename, email, child ID,

25
00:01:18.913 --> 00:01:20.014
or action endpoint.

26
00:01:20.094 --> 00:01:24.369
Determine whether the service requires
authorization context or treats it as

27
00:01:24.449 --> 00:01:25.032
optional.

28
00:01:25.112 --> 00:01:26.926
Review background jobs, caches,

29
00:01:27.006 --> 00:01:31.151
and download generation where
authorization can be lost after the handler.

30
00:01:31.231 --> 00:01:35.765
Log enough structured context to
investigate denials and suspicious cross-scope

31
00:01:35.845 --> 00:01:39.278
attempts without copying sensitive
contents or credentials.

32
00:01:39.358 --> 00:01:42.791
Protect those logs according to the
application's risk model.

33
00:01:42.871 --> 00:01:47.275
A deliberate choice between forbidden and
not found can limit object-existence

34
00:01:47.355 --> 00:01:48.067
disclosure.

35
00:01:48.147 --> 00:01:50.026
Use a consistent response policy,

36
00:01:50.106 --> 00:01:52.891
but do not confuse the status code with
the control.

37
00:01:52.971 --> 00:01:57.181
Returning four oh four after an unscoped
lookup may still expose or modify the

38
00:01:57.261 --> 00:01:58.362
object internally.

39
00:01:58.442 --> 00:02:01.033
The authorization decision, scoped data
path,

40
00:02:01.113 --> 00:02:03.898
and negative tests provide the security
property;

41
00:02:03.978 --> 00:02:06.180
the response code controls disclosure.
